; ======================================================================
;  hw_id.pi4 - Raspberry Pi 4 HwId backend: WHAT THIS COMPUTER IS.
;
;  Forum 575. The shared core used to state this board's facts as literal
;  prose - the banner said "the Raspberry Pi 4 serial monitor", named a
;  PL011 and asserted "exception level 2" - so the SECOND board printed
;  three claims that were all false about itself on the first line an
;  operator ever reads. The workaround was a board-local banner and a
;  board-local `version` on that target, which is a fork of the monitor's
;  own product text and would have to be written a third time for a third
;  board.
;
;  The cure is the rule every other family here already follows: the core
;  names no chip, and asks the board. This file is this board's answer.
;
;  NOTHING HERE IS A CAPABILITY. A board with no GPIO is a real board and
;  #CAP_GPIO says so; a board with no IDENTITY is not a thing, so there is
;  no #CAP_ID and no RequireCap gate. Every board supplies this seam whole,
;  the same way every board supplies HwAddr* (Anvil/Hal/hal.pbi).
;
;  INCLUDE ORDER: after Lib/mmu.pi4 (MmuEl), Board/memmap.pi4 (#MON_LO),
;  Core/format.pi4 (PutAddr), Anvil/Storage/hwfile.pbi and Board/storage.pi4
;  (gFatUp, HwFileOpen/HwFileSize/HwFileClose), and before
;  Core/flow_cmd.pi4 and Core/help.pi4, which are the two callers.
; ======================================================================

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  The three names. Short fragments the core prints inside its own
;  sentences, returned as addresses the same way HwAddrReason() and
;  HwI2cSourceWhere() return theirs.
;
;  HwIdBoard() is the PRODUCT name a person would use for the computer in
;  front of them, not a part number: the operator reading the banner is
;  identifying the board on their bench. HwIdCpu() is the core, because
;  the sentence it lands in is about the processor. HwIdTarget() is the
;  word that follows `-t` on the compiler's command line, and it is the
;  one that has to be exactly right - it is a checkable claim about how
;  the image was produced, and
;  somebody diagnosing a bad image goes where it points.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i HwIdBoard()
  ProcedureReturn "Raspberry Pi 4"
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwIdCpu()
  ProcedureReturn "Cortex-A72"
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwIdTarget()
  ProcedureReturn "pi4"
EndProcedure

; PMFBOOT v2's compiler-owned target identity. Numeric because it is an
; admission ABI rather than display text; 2711 is the append-only BCM2711
; target code declared by the compiler and Anvil/Core/pmfboot.pbi.
Procedure.i HwPmfTargetId()
  ProcedureReturn 2711
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  HwIdEl() - the exception level, READ FROM THE PROCESSOR.
;
;  The banner used to print the digit 2 as prose. It happened to be right
;  here and was wrong on the other board, and a number that is right by
;  luck is the shape of confident-wrong-answer this monitor exists to
;  refuse. MmuEl() (RaspberryPi4/Lib/mmu.pi4) decodes CurrentEL bits 3:2,
;  so the banner now prints what the running processor says it is - which
;  on this board is 2, because the armstub erets kernel8.img to EL2h, and
;  which would change on its own if a future boot path handed us EL1.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i HwIdEl()
  ProcedureReturn MmuEl()
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  HwIdSayConsole() - the console paragraph, PRINTED by the board.
;
;  A returned fragment would not do: what a console IS takes several
;  sentences and one of them carries an address. HwAddrReport() and
;  HwStorageReport() are the precedent - a board says its own multi-line
;  facts and the core decides only where in the output they go.
;
;  #UART_BASE, not #UART_DR. This said #UART_DR until 2026-08-26 and was
;  the right number for the wrong reason: the data register is at offset
;  $00, so "the DR address" and "the block base" happened to be the same
;  word. uart.pi4 keeps ONE base plus offsets now, so the register
;  constant is $00 and the base is what this line means. The printed
;  value does not change.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure HwIdSayConsole()
  Print("The serial port is the PL011 at ")
  PutAddr(#UART_BASE)
  PrintN(", set up from cold to 115200 baud,")
  PrintN("eight data bits, no parity, one stop bit.")
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  HwIdSayImage() - what KIND of image this is, printed inside `version`.
;
;  The other board's `version` had to explain that its image is an AArch64
;  UEFI application entered with boot services up. That is a real fact and
;  it is board knowledge, so it belongs behind this seam rather than in a
;  second copy of the command. This board's answer is the other half of
;  the same sentence, and it is worth saying out loud for the same reason:
;  it tells somebody holding a file which loader is supposed to take it.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure HwIdSayImage()
  PrintN("  This image is a kernel8.img. The firmware loads the whole file to")
  Print("  ")
  PutAddr(#MON_LO)
  PrintN(" and enters it there, with no loader in between -")
  PrintN("  which is why its size is not capped by anything: there is nothing")
  PrintN("  above it in the boot path to make room for.")
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  HwIdImageLen() - how many bytes this monitor's own image is, or 0.
;
;  Forum 586: two images could not be told apart at the prompt. The build
;  number says WHICH build; this is what lets `version` say WHAT is in it,
;  because with a length the monitor can run its own crc32 over its own
;  code and hand back a number the host can compute independently.
;
;  THE LENGTH IS NOT A CONSTANT AND CANNOT BE ONE - the image grows with
;  every commit. Until 2026-09-08 the only place on this board that knew
;  it was the FILE the firmware booted, so this opened KERNEL8.IMG and
;  read its size; the paragraph that used to be here explained why that
;  was worth a directory lookup and what to type when the medium was not
;  mounted yet.
;
;  IT NEEDS NO MEDIUM ANY MORE. The image carries the address of its own
;  end (RaspberryPi4/EmitCore_A64.pbi emits __image_start__ /
;  __image_end__, so that the monitor's extent can be the monitor rather
;  than a two-megabyte constant), and HwMonBytes() in Board/memmap.pi4 is
;  that measurement. So this answers from the running program: never 0,
;  never the size of a DIFFERENT image somebody has since written to the
;  stick, and no disk spun up to answer a question.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i HwIdImageBase()
  ; THE RUNNING BASE, MEASURED - not #MON_LO. It was that constant until
  ; 2026-09-08, on the argument that "the firmware always loads the boot
  ; image to the first address and the build is linked for it, so nobody
  ; else has a say". True of a boot from the stick and FALSE of a monitor
  ; relocated with --load-addr for a live-bench swap, which is a
  ; documented thing to do here - and the self-hash below would then have
  ; been computed over an address range this image is not at.
  ProcedureReturn HwMonLo()
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwIdImageLen()
  ; THE IMAGE ANSWERS THIS ITSELF NOW - 2026-09-08, and it is a straight
  ; deletion rather than a repair.
  ;
  ; This used to mount the boot medium and read the length of
  ; KERNEL8.IMG, because - as the note above this procedure said in as
  ; many words - "nothing in the built image records its own size". That
  ; stopped being true the day the toolchain started emitting
  ; __image_start__ / __image_end__ so that the monitor could compute its
  ; own extent instead of living inside a two-megabyte constant. The size
  ; is the same measurement HwMonBytes() makes, and taking it here means:
  ;
  ;   it cannot be 0 any more     - there is no "the medium is not
  ;                                 mounted yet, type ls first" answer to
  ;                                 give, so `version` self-hashes on the
  ;                                 first line an operator types.
  ;   it cannot be the WRONG file - reading KERNEL8.IMG off the stick
  ;                                 measured whatever is on the stick NOW,
  ;                                 which after an `anvil_update.py` run
  ;                                 is the NEXT monitor, not this one. The
  ;                                 self-hash was over the running image
  ;                                 and its length came from a different
  ;                                 image, and nothing said so.
  ;   it costs nothing            - two adrp/add pairs against a
  ;                                 directory lookup.
  ProcedureReturn HwMonBytes()
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  HwIdSayImageWhere() - the PROVENANCE of the number above, or the
;  reason there is not one. One procedure, two jobs, and the core calls
;  it either way, because "where did that come from" and "why is that
;  missing" are the same question asked of a board that may or may not be
;  able to answer.
;
;  IT PRINTS RATHER THAN RETURNING A FRAGMENT, for the same reason
;  HwIdSayConsole() does: the answer runs to more than one line on at
;  least one board, and a returned fragment would arrive as a single
;  line far past the width everything else here wraps at. It indents its
;  own lines to two spaces, which is what every sub-line of `version`
;  uses.
;
;  CALL IT AFTER HwIdImageLen(), not before - the same ordering rule
;  HwI2cSourceWhere() carries, and for the same reason.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure HwIdSayImageWhere()
  PrintN("  That extent is the RUNNING IMAGE's own: the compiler marks the end")
  PrintN("  of the image with a symbol and this program reads it, so the length")
  PrintN("  is of the code that is executing and not of a file on a disk that")
  PrintN("  may since have been replaced. It is also why this monitor has no")
  PrintN("  maximum size - type map.")
EndProcedure
